    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "An essential component of development and management of a fractured reservoir is the basic understanding of the fracture system and its effect on the storage and flow of hydrocarbons. The primary focus of this project is to develop and apply an integrative approach, employing both geological and geophysical methods, to fracture characterization in the Antelope Shale. The fracture system and its relation to hydrocarbon productivity in the Antelope Shale, a siliceous shale member of the Miocene Monterey Formation in the Buena Vista Hills (BVH) field, San Joaquin Valley, California, is poorly understood. Understanding gained from an integrative fracture characterization study in the Antelope Shale will aid in future reservoir management plans for the BVH field, and will also serve as an analog for other siliceous shale reservoirs in the Monterey Formation in both on shore and offshore fields.\r\n\r\nThe objectives of this study are (1) to integrate surface data with subsurface data to identify effective hydrocarbon pathways and characterize their geometry; (2) to employ Formation Microscanner (FMS) data in conjunction with core at the same interval to characterize subsurface fractures; and\r\nfinally (3) to analyze dipmeter data around the Buena Vista Hills field to constrain the in situ stress field to determine its influence on hydraulic conductivity and possibly its relationship to the formation of the hydrocarbon-carrying fractures.\r\n\r\nPreliminary results from both surface outcrops and the subsurface suggest that brecciated-fault zones and associated fracture systems are the most significant conduits for hydrocarbon transport. Work is in progress to understand the formation of these structures and to predict their locations in folded reservoirs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
